# PRD: Independent planned URs, backlog retirement and optional Codex planning

Status: draft
Gate: PRD
Gate approval: open
Based on: UR
Date: 2026-10-31
Owner: Arndt Gold
Traceability contract: criteria-chain-v1

## 1. Product Scope

Deliver a complete path from a future need to one explicitly started Core Run, without a mandatory shared MASTER_BACKLOG.md. A maintainer can keep independently editable UR drafts under .agdf/control/planned/, inspect them alongside canonical Runs, and deliberately hand a selected source version to Core. Planned work is limited to a UR and minimal steering metadata. Active delivery keeps existing Run/artefact paths, gates and approval authority.

The initial optional product is AGDF Planning, delivered as a separately installable/enabled Codex plugin requiring compatible AGDF Core. It organizes capture, clarification, refinement and prioritization through the existing ur-definition planning mode; it has no second UR specification, gate engine or status store. Core owns the hostneutral planned-entry and handoff contract and remains useful without this plugin: inspect/validate planned entries, invoke the shared UR planning mode and explicitly start a Run, or create a direct Run without planned input. No broad extension platform is introduced.

Core, CLI/MCP and existing Cockpit consumers must use compatible meanings derived from individual planning entries and canonical Runs. Human-readable titles, priority and origin references remain visible. Active and ended Runs must not disappear merely because a legacy backlog row is absent.

A planned entry has a stable identity, a readable title, priority and canonical UR content; relevant source links and unanswered clarification remain with that undertaking. Priority has three product values high, normal and low, default normal, affecting planning order only. Exact encoding, validation schema and deterministic tie ordering belong to SD. There is no mandatory independent metadata/status database or mutable global index. Incomplete needs may be saved as drafts and visibly remain incomplete.

Successful handoff retains the selected draft as historical/reference material with a traceable Run association. Subsequent active UR changes belong to Core; planning must not silently synchronize or overwrite approved content. The same planning identity cannot silently create another Run after successful handoff. A genuinely separate undertaking uses a new explicit identity.

Legacy cutover accounts for every backlog row and its original text, priority, links and history. It associates uniquely evidenced existing Runs, creates unapproved planned material only where supported, or retains an explicit unresolved item. It does not invent missing requirements, associations or approvals. A preserved read-only legacy snapshot may remain as evidence; ordinary new-model operation never requires or rewrites a live master backlog.

## 2. UX Intent And Success

- ui_ux_impact: medium
- ux_intent_definition: ready; UX_INTENT_DEFINITION.md in this Run, digest sha256:eb6f444b09241da7a24b6a1e008b7af24bde930d37fcc6ce64f472db66f5b2a5
- primary_user_intent: Plan and clarify one future need independently, then deliberately start its traceable governed delivery.
- success_signal: A user can find the undertaking by readable title, distinguish planned from active/ended work, edit one draft without changing another, and locate either the resulting Run or a concrete recovery action.
- primary_decision_or_action: Refine or prioritize a draft, or explicitly start one Run from its displayed selected version.

The UX report's agent-only written walkthrough covers capture, Core-only navigation, stale selection, interrupted handoff, unresolved legacy rows and historical origin after handoff. Its proposed behavior is adopted below in the stable criteria. No interactive preview was required by the UR, created or observed; no human usability observation is claimed. PRD approval adopts the specified behavior and criteria, not a claim of implementation, host qualification or completed acceptance.

## 3. Working Modes And Effective State

| working_mode | effective_state | visible_state_types | effective_state_authority | primary_state_presentation_owner |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Codex optional planning dialogue | Unapproved UR draft and local steering metadata | Draft, incomplete/invalid input, saved current version, handed-off reference | Selected planned UR/version and Core validation | AGDF Planning dialogue using shared ur-definition |
| Core without optional plugin | Same planning facts; deliberate start remains available | Planned, unavailable/partial, explicit handoff outcome, direct Run | Planned source, verified handoff evidence and canonical Run | Existing Core CLI/MCP result and Cockpit overview/detail |
| Combined overview and detail | Derived planning and actual Run lifecycle | Planned draft, handed-off reference, active Run, ended Run, invalid/unavailable source | Individual planning sources and canonical Runs, with explicit partial-read limits | Existing shared overview/detail surfaces |
| Handoff and recovery | One concrete source/operation under transfer | Pending, conflict, uncertain, failed, completed with Run link | Actual original operation and source-bound Core result | Core handoff result as presented by the invoking surface |
| Active Run delivery | Current gate, source revision and lifecycle | Actual gate/blocker, approval pending, completed/closed | Existing canonical Run and registered artefacts | Existing Core Run/gate presentation |
| Legacy reconciliation | Evidenced association or unresolved historical material | Accounted row, incomplete planned need, linked Run/history, ambiguous association, pending recovery | Preserved legacy facts plus independently evidenced current sources | Existing migration result and overview/detail |

These state labels are user-visible meaning, not instructions to persist a duplicate status registry.

## 4. Activation, Blockers, Recovery And Transitions

- activation_and_deactivation: Planning begins only from an explicit user request to capture/refine/prioritize a need or open an entry for that purpose. Discovery, installation, list selection and priority do not start delivery. Explicit start names the displayed source version. Ending the dialogue preserves saved draft material; disabling/removing the optional plugin does not delete planned entries or Runs.
- blockers_and_visible_next_actions: Show affected identity/source and a concrete next action for invalid UR/metadata, duplicate identity, unsupported version, stale selection, unsafe/unavailable input, uncertain prior handoff and incompatible pending legacy recovery. Invalid planning entries do not prohibit unrelated valid Run work. A pending operation may block genuinely affected shared resources; its scope and reason must be explicit.
- recovery_paths: Correct incomplete/invalid drafts through the shared UR owner. Refresh and deliberately reselect changed material. Inspect the original uncertain operation before retry/recovery; reuse its identity and selected version. Expose retry for transient reads. Preserve unresolved legacy material for explicit reconciliation. Do not advise a second unbound Run creation to evade uncertainty.
- relevant_state_transitions: Need -> saved unapproved draft; explicit edit -> new draft version; explicit start of selected version -> pending handoff -> one Run awaiting ordinary UR review and approval, with origin link. Changed source before commitment -> visible conflict, no silent substitution. Lost response -> inspect/recover original operation -> same Run or concrete no-effect/failure result. Completed handoff -> historical planning reference plus live Run navigation. Legacy row -> accounted planned/Run/history disposition or explicit unresolved input. Failed read -> unavailable/partial, never an apparently empty successful result.

## 5. Acceptance Criteria

### Core operation without a live master backlog

- criterion_id: AC-001
- requirement: A new-model repository initializes, creates/updates/approves/revises/closes ordinary Runs and performs required inspection without requiring or rewriting a live MASTER_BACKLOG.md. Initialization provides the planned area and canonical Run facilities. Historical retained backlog bytes are not another live authority.
- observable_success: The covered operations complete with the same applicable gate/approval protections when the live file is absent; no operation recreates it as a mandatory projection.
- required_evidence: Affected initialization, doctor/readiness, writer, revision, closure and packaging checks against repositories with absent live backlog and preserved optional historical material.

### Independent planned drafts and minimal metadata

- criterion_id: AC-002
- working_mode: Shared UR planning mode with or without optional Codex orchestration.
- source_state: No entry, or an editable unapproved entry.
- action: Explicitly capture, revise or reprioritize one selected undertaking.
- requirement: Save canonical UR content and minimal identity/title/priority metadata under that entry; no Run, PRD, SD or TP is required. Use high/normal/low priority, default normal. Entry content is the authority for those values.
- expected_effective_state: One current unapproved planned draft; another entry and unrelated Run remain unchanged.
- visible_feedback: Readable title, stable identity, saved version, priority and remaining clarification.
- failure_behavior: Invalid/duplicate identity, invalid priority, unsafe path or stale expected version rejects the affected write without partial replacement or false success.
- recovery: Correct the named field or refresh/reselect the actual current draft. An incomplete need can be saved and remains visibly incomplete.
- observable_success: Independent entries can be refined without updating a shared writable list; the saved title/priority/UR can be inspected directly and through Core.
- required_evidence: Positive capture/edit/priority cases, incomplete draft, invalid/stale/duplicate/unsafe negatives and unchanged unrelated-file observations.

### One UR owner and no planning authority escalation

- criterion_id: AC-003
- requirement: Planning and active UR authoring use the same canonical UR structure and semantic rules through ur-definition. Planning is explicitly available before a Run exists and requires no fabricated Run/revision identifiers. Planning results confer no gate approval, delivery authorization or hidden PRD/SD/TP.
- observable_success: The same relevant UR content is accepted or diagnosed consistently in both modes; saved planning remains unapproved and starting delivery still requires normal Core UR presentation/approval.
- required_evidence: Shared-template/contract and planning-without-Run tests, mode-specific writer guards and no implicit approval/Run-creation negative cases.

### Readable shared overview and detail

- criterion_id: AC-004
- working_mode: Core, CLI/MCP and existing Cockpit overview/detail.
- source_state: Mixed valid planned entries, active/ended Runs and handed-off references.
- action: Read, filter or open an item.
- requirement: Derive membership/state from the relevant sources, show readable undertaking title, identity, priority where applicable and origin/Run link; distinguish draft from actual lifecycle/gate and show canonical Runs even without backlog membership.
- expected_effective_state: Read-only view of the selected source; no delivery or approval.
- visible_feedback: Clearly labeled planned, handed-off, active or ended meaning and source detail; equivalent facts across affected surfaces.
- failure_behavior: A partial/unavailable/invalid read is visibly different from a successful empty inventory; one failed item does not masquerade as a valid state or silently erase the rest.
- recovery: Retry transient read, inspect the affected source or open the canonical Run. Last-known values, if shown, are explicitly stale.
- observable_success: Users can determine what they are viewing and its permitted next action without a writable global summary.
- required_evidence: Shared Core projection and adapter parity tests, missing/partial/unsafe/empty cases and actual visible Codex Cockpit observations for applicable behavior.

### Optional Codex planning product and Core autonomy

- criterion_id: AC-005
- working_mode: Codex with AGDF Planning enabled; Core without that package.
- source_state: Compatible Core installed, optional plugin present or absent.
- action: Explicitly request capture, clarification, refinement, prioritization or start.
- requirement: Deliver a separately installable/enabled optional Codex planning package using the shared UR mode and Core contract. Core-only users retain inspection/validation, shared UR planning mode, explicit source-bound handoff and direct new-Run creation.
- expected_effective_state: The same draft and Run facts regardless of optional orchestration; planning plugin owns dialogue organization only.
- visible_feedback: Clear invoked mode and required compatible Core; missing optional plugin does not imply lost data or disabled Core.
- failure_behavior: Incompatible/missing Core yields a specific dependency/version diagnosis before mutation. Removing/disabling the extension preserves sources and Runs.
- recovery: Use Core directly or install/enable a compatible optional package deliberately.
- observable_success: A complete Codex planning-to-Run flow works with the plugin; Core-only cases work without it, using identical semantics.
- required_evidence: Package/install/discovery/negative dependency checks, native Codex invocation and Core-only comparison on exact candidate versions; no broader-host support inferred.

### Deliberate version-bound handoff

- criterion_id: AC-006
- working_mode: Explicit start from planned work.
- source_state: A selected current draft eligible for ordinary Core UR preparation.
- action: Deliberately start a Run from the identified source version.
- requirement: Bind the actual selected content and origin to one resulting Run, retain the planning source and require normal Core UR review/approval. Incomplete requirements never bypass the existing readiness/clarification owner.
- expected_effective_state: One traceable Run with the selected UR content awaiting ordinary Core control; the retained draft is reference material.
- visible_feedback: Run identity/link, selected source/version, origin and the next actual UR/gate action.
- failure_behavior: Changed, invalid or unavailable source fails visibly before silent substitution or a falsely completed handoff.
- recovery: Refresh/reselect or correct the source; inspect actual operation outcome after uncertainty.
- observable_success: The Run's initial content/origin match the deliberate selection; no approval is inherited from planning.
- required_evidence: Source-bound positive and stale/unsafe/incomplete cases, exact origin/content comparison and native Codex flow.

### Idempotence, interruption and historical ownership

- criterion_id: AC-007
- working_mode: Handoff retry/recovery and subsequent navigation.
- source_state: Pending/uncertain operation or already completed transfer.
- action: Inspect/retry/recover the original operation, or open its result.
- requirement: Replaying the same operation yields the same Run/effect; another request must not silently create a second Run for an already transferred planning identity. Preserve selected content, source history and the active Run's authority. Later scope changes use existing Core revision rules.
- expected_effective_state: One resolved transfer or explicit unresolved failure with retained evidence; completed source becomes historical/reference material.
- visible_feedback: Original operation, known/unknown effect, same Run link on success, and precise eligible next action.
- failure_behavior: Source/revision conflict, interrupted commit or incompatible recovery never loses the source or fabricates completion.
- recovery: Inspect and use the supported recovery/retry of the original operation. A genuinely different undertaking requires an explicit new planning identity.
- observable_success: Fault-injection and concurrent duplicate-start cases converge to at most one Run; approved active UR bytes remain protected from planning edits.
- required_evidence: Commit-boundary/lost-response/replay/concurrent handoff tests and visible recoverable feedback in the initial Codex flow.

### Lossless and explicit legacy disposition

- criterion_id: AC-008
- working_mode: Migration/reconciliation and its resulting overview.
- source_state: Existing planning, active, archive/history or ambiguous backlog rows.
- action: Preview and explicitly apply supported cutover/reconciliation.
- requirement: Account for every original row and preserve its text, priority, references and relevant history, with an evidenced planned-entry/Run/history disposition or an explicit unresolved item. Existing Run/artefact paths, approvals and protected historical bytes remain valid; missing needs or associations are never invented.
- expected_effective_state: Traceable new-model entries/Run associations plus inspectable unresolved historical input; no mandatory writable backlog.
- visible_feedback: Per-item disposition, unresolved reasons and affected recovery restrictions; distinguish an incomplete migrated need from an accepted UR.
- failure_behavior: Ambiguity, unsupported legacy form or pending incompatible journal preserves original material and prevents unsafe affected cutover; no silent deletion or guessed match.
- recovery: Inspect preserved original facts and deliberately reconcile the affected item or finish the supported legacy recovery first.
- observable_success: Complete row accounting and byte/source preservation can be demonstrated; interrupted retry neither drops nor duplicates work.
- required_evidence: Representative real-repository input preview, isolated migration fixtures including orphan/duplicate/history/custom text and interrupted retry, plus pre/post approval/path integrity checks. Real in-place migration requires its own explicit user action.

### Independent progress with retained true invariants

- criterion_id: AC-009
- requirement: Independent planned-entry and Run operations do not acquire or depend on a live master-backlog write/lock or another unrelated Run's backlog-only pending journal. Preserve same-entry/Run revision checks, idempotence, approval integrity and genuinely shared-resource recovery exclusions.
- observable_success: Controlled concurrent cases show independent operations progressing without shared-backlog contention, while conflicting same-resource work fails/retries safely. Every remaining project-wide exclusion has a named actual invariant and bounded effect.
- required_evidence: Targeted concurrent processes and interruption tests covering both independence and retained conflicts; source analysis of all affected writers/locks/recovery paths. No numeric performance claim without measurements; shared Git/branch operations are outside this concurrency promise.

### Visible diagnosis and recovery consistency

- criterion_id: AC-010
- working_mode: Planning, inventory, handoff and migration across affected adapters.
- source_state: Invalid, stale, unsafe, partial/unavailable or uncertain state.
- action: Inspect the failure and take its offered next action.
- requirement: Explain the concrete cause, affected source and eligible recovery; preserve uncertainty and original operation identity without interpreting source text as executable instructions.
- expected_effective_state: Safe diagnosed state or verified recovery, never implicit approval/start.
- visible_feedback: Distinct empty/partial/unavailable and pending/failed/completed meanings, with visible retry for recoverable transient failure.
- failure_behavior: Unsafe or unauthorized operations remain rejected; technical IDs supplement a readable task name rather than substituting for explanation.
- recovery: Correct, refresh, retry read, inspect original operation or reconcile the named legacy item as applicable.
- observable_success: Equivalent underlying conditions produce compatible explanations/next actions in Core, CLI/MCP, Codex dialogue and applicable Cockpit views.
- required_evidence: Condition matrix with positive recovery and negative authority cases, plus native/visible observations for the initial supported flow.

### Compatibility, evidence and maintainable delivery

- criterion_id: AC-011
- requirement: Update affected contracts, scaffolds, generated distributions, help/install documentation and existing consumers coherently. State compatible control/runtime versions and reject unsupported writer combinations concretely. Qualify the optional planning package initially for Codex only; preserve existing other-host Core workflows within their stated support boundaries.
- observable_success: Required affected checks and CI pass for the candidate; installation/discovery and actual invocation of the exact Codex candidate are evidenced; existing Core operation without optional planning remains supported. No repository test is reported as human acceptance or unobserved host support.
- required_evidence: Mandatory PR/merge checks plus affected targeted tests, generated-asset parity, exact-version installed CLI/MCP/Codex and visible Cockpit evidence. SD/TP identify other-host compatibility obligations and qualify only actual observed claims. Human UAT remains a separate gate.

## 6. Non-Goals

No mandatory optional-plugin installation, generic extension framework, second UR author, PRD/SD/TP in planned, new gate system, implicit approval, automatic implementation/start from selection or priority, global writable index, remote planning service, calendar, new multi-user permissions system, bulk source deletion, automatic release/merge or blanket removal of all locks. New native planning-plugin support for hosts other than Codex is deferred.

## 7. Users And Roles

Maintainers and contributing agents capture/refine needs and inspect actual state. Arndt Gold is PRD/product decision owner and retains deliberate gate authority. The optional Codex planning dialogue facilitates clarification; it cannot settle human approvals. Core remains the delivery-control authority. Existing review/QA/maintainer responsibilities in CONTRIBUTING.md and GOVERNANCE.md remain applicable. A planning source or plugin grants no merge, release or roadmap commitment.

## 8. Constraints

Use the dedicated worktree/branch and approved UR scope. Reuse current Core, UR and presentation owners. Existing paths and approvals cannot be rewritten to manufacture migration success. New planning authoring requires no fake Run binding; active-Run writers retain their existing protections. Untrusted source contents are data. No hidden sync back from an active Run to its historical draft. Preserve legacy facts and source provenance.

Distinguish safe concurrent control-file work from Git/index/branch conflicts or divergent worktree copies. Same-Run conflicts across versions/worktrees remain subject to actual source/revision semantics. The product promises removal of artificial backlog coupling, not distributed coordination or contention-free Git merging.

The prerequisite UX-template routing repair already made in this worktree is separately documented in RUNTIME_REPAIR.md. Its targeted tests and local installation observations do not prove this planned-work feature. Current gates for the feature remain in force.

## 9. Evidence Requirements

UR -> PRD mapping: UR AC-01 maps to AC-001; AC-02 to AC-002/004; AC-03 to AC-003; AC-04 to AC-004/010; AC-05 to AC-005/011; AC-06 to AC-006/007; AC-07 to AC-008; AC-08 to AC-009; AC-09 to AC-011. UX walkthrough 1 maps to AC-002/003/005, 2 to AC-004/010, 3 to AC-006, 4 to AC-007/010, 5 to AC-008/010, and 6 to AC-007.

SD must map every stable criterion exactly once to design/SoT ownership, decisions, compatibility and risk treatment. TP must cover those mappings with executable positive/negative/recovery/concurrency scenarios and evidence owners. Prefer reuse of applicable proof with exact candidate/environment and limits; later stage passage alone does not require repeated execution.

Mandatory evidence includes affected repository/CI checks; actual installed Codex plugin discovery, planning-mode invocation, Core-only operation and handoff on the same candidate; visible applicable Cockpit states and recovery; migration losslessness and protected approval integrity; independence plus same-resource conflict/interruption behavior. Exact client/runtime/version/OS bindings accompany native evidence. UI correctness needs visible observations; semantic/source tests alone are insufficient. Other hosts get no expanded native claim from adapter tests.

At this drafting stage only approved sources, Brownfield source inspection, recorded UX analysis and user scope clarification exist. No feature implementation, migration, new host support, quantitative contention improvement or human acceptance has been observed. UR preview requirement is none; the written UX scenario walkthrough is agent analysis, not a tested preview.

## 10. Risks And Open Questions

Persistent planning and legacy journals require a coherent cutover, not a moved table. A mandatory shared index, duplicated UR author or automatic active/planned sync would recreate the bottleneck or authority conflict. Unclear migration associations must remain visible. Pending legacy recovery and old runtimes must not mutate new state unsafely. A missing optional plugin must not strand existing data.

No unresolved before-PRD product question remains. Exact schemas/encoding, ownership boundaries in code, transaction protocol, backward-version admission and package wiring remain named SD obligations. Test mechanisms and native qualification execution remain TP obligations. Failure to resolve their invariant/compatibility consequences before their gates blocks progression; it is not accepted technical debt.

